    <div class="article-width">
      <header class="article-header">
        <span class="article-category">OUR BRAND STORY</span>
        <h2>Our Story</h2>
        <div class="article-meta"><span>By Deep Tanzania Tours</span><span>Founded in 2024 by Benson Nyamataga</span></div>
      </header>
      <div class="article-body">
        <div class="intro-quote"><blockquote>“Travel Deep. Go Beyond.”</blockquote></div>
        <p>Deep Tanzania Tours began in 2024, but the idea behind it goes back to our founder&#x27;s years travelling between Mara and Moshi. Benson Nyamataga grew up in Tanzania&#x27;s Mara Region, close to the landscapes of the Serengeti. Later, while studying information technology in Moshi, he found himself looking at Mount Kilimanjaro and wondering about the people who came from around the world to climb it.</p>
<p>He did not set out to become a tour operator. At first, he was simply curious: how do travellers organise a safari, choose the right park, find their guides and decide where to stay? Over time, those questions became an interest in the whole experience of travelling through Tanzania, not only the famous places on an itinerary.</p>
        <div class="highlight-box"><p><strong>Our approach:</strong> a good Tanzania journey is about more than reaching a destination. It is about choosing the right route, giving each place enough time and helping travellers connect with the wildlife, landscapes and people they came to experience.</p></div>
        <h2>How Deep Tanzania Tours Began</h2>
        <p>Benson was born in Mara, a region closely connected with the Serengeti ecosystem and the Mara River. The river is especially known for the dramatic crossings that can take place during the Great Migration. For Benson, though, the Serengeti first became familiar through ordinary journeys home. During university holidays, he travelled from Moshi to Mara by local transport, sometimes passing through the park. Along the road he could see wildlife, as well as visitors in safari vehicles stopping to watch animals and take photographs.</p>
<p>He remembers being struck by the difference between their journeys. He was travelling home; they had come a long way to see a place that was part of his own country. He wanted to know who arranged their trips, how the safari vehicles worked, why one route was chosen over another and what made a day in the bush special for a visitor. Those questions stayed with him.</p>
<p>In 2019, Benson began studying for his bachelor&#x27;s degree in information technology at Moshi Co-operative University. From around the university and Ushirika Stadium, he could see Kilimanjaro on clear days. Moshi was a different introduction to tourism. Here, the mountain was part of daily life, but it was also the reason so many travellers arrived in town. Benson became interested in the planning behind a climb: choosing a route, allowing time to adjust to altitude, arranging guides and porters, checking equipment and preparing for the days on the mountain.</p>

        <p>His first practical connection to tourism came through website design. While still a student, he built websites for local tour operators. At the beginning, he knew more about creating a page than explaining a safari properly. Writing about a destination was one thing; understanding park entrances, realistic driving times, accommodation locations, transfers and the order of a good itinerary was another. He spent time reading, researching and asking questions because he wanted the information he shared with travellers to be useful, not simply attractive.</p>
<p>After graduating in 2022, Benson continued working as a freelance web designer and learning digital marketing. That same year, he joined a Tanzanian tour operator in an operations and digital marketing role. This gave him the opportunity to see how trips were actually put together, from the first enquiry to the journey on the ground. He joined safari trips, spoke with guests, spent time with guides and learned what could make a plan work well—or become tiring when too much was squeezed into one day.</p>
<p>Visits to Tarangire, Ngorongoro, Serengeti and Lake Manyara brought the details to life. Tarangire has its elephant country and baobab scenery. Ngorongoro offers a very different kind of game drive inside the crater. The Serengeti is vast, and where you stay can make a real difference to how much time you spend looking for wildlife instead of driving between camps. Lake Manyara adds another landscape and a different pace. Benson learned that choosing the parks is only the beginning. The season, number of nights, driving distances, guide&#x27;s local knowledge and accommodation all shape the experience.</p>
<p>Just as important were conversations with travellers and local people. Guests do not all want the same safari: some hope to spend hours looking for big cats, some want photography time, others are travelling with children or would rather have a slower day. Spending time with guides and communities also showed Benson why cultural visits, village life and local stories deserve their own place in a Tanzania holiday. He came to believe that listening carefully was as important as knowing the map.</p>
<p>In 2024, he founded Deep Tanzania Tours. The first trips were close to home: outings around Moshi and Arusha, waterfall visits, time with local communities and day safaris to Tarangire and Ngorongoro. It was a practical beginning, built on the places he knew and the experience he had gained. The aim was never to offer the longest possible list of stops. It was to organise journeys people could enjoy at a comfortable pace, with good information before they travelled.</p>
        <h2>What “Travel Deep. Go Beyond.” Means to Us</h2>
        <p>That is where our name comes from. To us, travelling deep means taking time to understand a place rather than passing through it. A safari in the Serengeti should leave room for an unhurried game drive. A visit to Ngorongoro should be planned around the journey into the crater, not just its name on a schedule. A Kilimanjaro trek should be approached with respect for the mountain, the crew and the time needed to prepare. And for travellers adding Zanzibar, the beach can be more than a final hotel stop: it can be a chance to experience another side of Tanzania through its coast, culture and people.</p>
<p>Today, Deep Tanzania Tours focuses on planning Tanzania safaris, Kilimanjaro adventures, cultural and local experiences, and Zanzibar holiday extensions around what each traveller actually wants. We pay attention to the smaller decisions: which parks fit the number of days available, whether a route involves too much driving, what type of accommodation feels right and where there is space to slow down. Those details are rarely the first thing people see in a holiday photograph, but they make a difference once the journey begins.</p>
<p>Benson&#x27;s story started with a view of Kilimanjaro and a journey through the Serengeti. Our hope is that when you travel with us, you come away with something of that same curiosity: more than photographs, more than a list of parks, and a better sense of the Tanzania we are proud to call home.</p>
        <p class="closing-motto">Travel Deep. Go Beyond.</p>
